WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.958
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Active
Assist is a collection

00:00:01.958 --> 00:00:04.050
of tools and services that
simplify your resource

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:06.270
management experience
on Google Cloud.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:07.865
In this video, we're
going to show you

00:00:07.865 --> 00:00:09.240
three recommenders
that will help

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.150
you zero in on the opportunities
to save on the costs

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.770
operating in Google Cloud--

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:18.300
the Idle Virtual Machine, or
VM recommender, the VM machine

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.870
type recommender, and the
unattended project recommender.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:24.380
Let's get into it.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.547
On Google Cloud Compute
Engine, you can spin up

00:00:26.547 --> 00:00:28.880
virtual machines, and you'll
only be billed for the time

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.140
that each is running.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.600
This pay-as-you-go model
is one of the best benefits

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.000
of building your
infrastructure in the Cloud.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.740
At the same time,
if your project

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:40.160
has lots of virtual machines,
costs can add up over time.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:41.880
You'll be paying
for those resources,

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.570
whether you're actively
using them or not.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:47.120
To avoid paying for resources
that you're not using,

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.430
there's two things you
want to make sure of.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.910
First, ensure that every machine
that's running in your project

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:54.557
is being put to good use.

00:00:54.557 --> 00:00:57.140
And second, make sure that the
virtual machines you're running

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.600
have the appropriate
resource allocations,

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.400
such as the number
of virtual CPUs,

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:02.960
and the size of the memory.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.760
For example, you'll
probably like

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.400
to know whether the VM running
in your development environment

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.895
is still being used, or
if it was just a test

00:01:09.895 --> 00:01:11.270
machine that
someone accidentally

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.370
forgot to turn off or delete.

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:15.470
And if it is being
used, you want

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.810
to be confident that it has
just the right resources

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.640
configured for the
intended workload.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.000
You could do an audit yourself
to figure these things out,

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.390
but if you're looking
at number of projects,

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:29.090
each with multiple virtual
machines, multiple users,

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:31.850
teams, applications,
this can quickly become

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:34.470
a labor-intensive approach.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.140
This is where a
couple of recommenders

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.600
can help drastically
simplify the experience

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.840
to help avoid paying for
things that you're not using.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.420
First, the Idle VM
recommender will

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.100
highlight VMs that
haven't recently

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.890
been used so you can shut
them down or delete them

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:50.420
from your project.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.460
The recommendations
for idle VMs are

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:54.350
based on system metrics
that are gathered

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.860
by the Cloud monitoring
service over the past 14 days.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:01.460
For new machines, this starts
a few days after creation.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.910
A VM is classified as idle when
all three of these criteria

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:05.960
are met--

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:11.420
CPU utilization is less than
0.03 for 97% of the VM runtime,

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.600
received network traffic is
less than 2,600 bytes per second

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.810
for 95% of the VM
runtime, and sent

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:20.390
network traffic is less
than 1,000 bytes per second

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:22.730
for 95% of the VM runtime.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.263
With these criteria met, a
VM is identified as idle.

00:02:26.263 --> 00:02:28.430
It's up to you to decide
whether to keep it running,

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:31.910
shut it down, or delete it
completely from your project.

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:34.513
If you shut it down,
it'll stop accruing costs.

00:02:34.513 --> 00:02:36.680
Plus, you can always start
it back up if you need it

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.280
again in the future.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.390
So that's idle VMs, but
how can Active Assist

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:44.330
help with costs of
machines that aren't idle?

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.400
Well, along with the
Idle VM recommender,

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.220
you'll also see recommendations
from the VM machine type

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:50.450
recommender.

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:51.950
These recommendations
are also known

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:54.440
as rightsizing recommendations,
because they're all

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.620
about finding the right
size for your workloads.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:59.690
Rightsizing recommendations
are generated

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:02.570
by monitoring the CPU and
memory utilization of running

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:05.060
VMs for the last eight days.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.040
This data helps to identify
if the machines are

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.830
not efficiently using all of
their allocated resources.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:11.810
With that, the
recommender can suggest

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:15.053
a more appropriate machine
type for that virtual machine.

00:03:15.053 --> 00:03:16.970
Let's say that you have
an application running

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:19.670
on an n2-standard-16 instance.

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:22.070
That machine type
has 16 virtual CPUs,

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:24.170
and 64 gigabytes of memory.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.810
If your application generally
uses most of the CPU,

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:30.052
but never really uses more
than 16 gigabytes of memory,

00:03:30.052 --> 00:03:31.760
you might get a
rightsized recommendation

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.970
to use an n2-highcpu-16 instead.

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:38.030
This machine type has the
same number of virtual CPUs,

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:41.990
but has a lot less memory, and
an overall lower hourly price.

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:43.520
When rightsizing
recommendations are

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.500
made for machines with
less resource usage

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:47.510
than they have
allocated, applying them

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:50.000
results in a reduction in
costs without impacting

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.373
your performance.

00:03:51.373 --> 00:03:53.540
The savings will multiply
with every virtual machine

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:57.080
that you're able to downsize
based on these recommendations.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.780
Virtual machines aren't the
only source of costs, though.

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:01.730
Other cost-cutting
recommenders include

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:04.640
the committed used discount
recommender, the idle custom

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.970
image recommender, the idle
IP address recommender,

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.820
and the idle persistent
disk recommender.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:14.270
Beyond those resources, the
unattended project recommender

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:16.250
will help you
identify projects that

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:20.070
are potentially unused so they
can be reviewed or deleted.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:21.740
This is especially
valuable if you're

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:24.140
an administrator for an
entire organization with lots

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:24.977
of projects.

00:04:24.977 --> 00:04:26.810
There's a good chance
some of those projects

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:29.480
could have Cloud resources
that are accruing costs,

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:31.850
even though they're not
actively being used.

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:34.640
The way it works is by
analyzing project usage data

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:36.320
over the last 30 days.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.090
For any project with low
usage in that period of time,

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:42.380
and for which there are no OAuth
tokens used in the last 180

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:44.300
days, you'll receive
a recommendation

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.430
to review or delete the project.

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:47.960
When you delete
a project, you're

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.570
also deleting all of the
resources in that project

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:52.520
that you're being billed for.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.220
This recommender also lets
you take a proactive approach

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:57.170
to managing projects
by identifying projects

00:04:57.170 --> 00:04:59.540
that still have usage,
but whose owners are not

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:01.212
active on the project.

00:05:01.212 --> 00:05:03.170
For those projects, you'll
get a recommendation

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:04.880
to reclaim the
project by assigning

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:06.218
someone else as the owner.

00:05:06.218 --> 00:05:08.510
This can help prevent projects
from becoming unattended

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:10.760
in the first place by making
sure that there's someone

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:12.570
accountable for each of them.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:15.650
Now, let me show you how simple
it is to discover and apply

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:17.750
some recommendations from
the Recommendation hub

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.340
with a few clicks.

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:22.640
In this scenario, I've got the
Compute Recommender Admin IAM

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:25.970
role assigned so that I can view
and manage idle VM and machine

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:27.410
type recommendations.

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:29.828
I also have the
Compute Admin IAM role

00:05:29.828 --> 00:05:31.370
so that I'm able to
apply the changes

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:33.080
in those recommendations.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:35.600
On top of those, I'm also
using the Cloud Spanner Viewer

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.900
role to view the CPU utilization
graphs included with the VM

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:42.360
machine type recommendations.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.340
Now that we're in the
Google Cloud Console,

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:46.320
the first place we might
notice recommendations

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:49.590
for our virtual machines is
the Compute Engine VM Instances

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:50.380
page.

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:53.070
This page will show all of the
virtual machines associated

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.042
with your project.

00:05:55.042 --> 00:05:56.750
There's a summary at
the top of the page,

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:58.970
and individual
recommendations next

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:01.570
to the corresponding instance.

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:03.510
We can see these same
recommendations over

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:05.780
in the Recommendation hub.

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:07.280
Let's start with
the recommendations

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:10.240
from the idle VM recommender.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:12.160
Here, in the list
of recommendations,

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:15.370
we'll see each recommendation,
the instance it corresponds to,

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:17.990
and the recommended action.

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:20.600
Directly from here, we can
click any recommendation

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:22.160
and perform the
recommended action--

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:25.450
in this case, shutting
down the instance.

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:27.660
If stopping the instance
isn't an option for us,

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:29.610
this machine has
another recommendation--

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:31.170
to switch the machine type.

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:33.905
Let's stop this VM for now.

00:06:33.905 --> 00:06:36.030
Let's take a look at the
recommendation from the VM

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:38.190
machine type recommender.

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:40.290
This time we'll see
the recommendation,

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:43.290
the corresponding instance,
the recommended machine type,

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:47.007
and the current machine type.

00:06:47.007 --> 00:06:48.840
When we click on one
of the recommendations,

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.670
we'll be able to see
a more complete view

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:53.350
of the recommended
configuration.

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:56.320
If we choose, we can adjust
the recommended configuration

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:59.600
and add more memory or cores.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:01.220
After reviewing
a recommendation,

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:02.780
if we decide it
isn't applicable,

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:04.670
we can simply dismiss
it to have it removed

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:06.350
from the recommendation
hub, and stop

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:09.070
receiving the recommendation.

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:10.930
Otherwise, we can accept
the recommendation

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:13.210
and apply it from here.

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:15.480
Note that this will shut
down the instance briefly,

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:17.400
so make sure there's
redundancy in place,

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:21.160
or that you're working within a
designated maintenance window.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.380
Once the instance restarts
in the new machine type,

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:24.970
the corresponding
recommendation will

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:27.940
be marked as applied in
your recommendation history.

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:29.650
All of the Active
Assist recommendations

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:32.140
we've talked about are designed
to highlight opportunities

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:34.570
to take action on things that
could be difficult to keep

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:36.760
track of otherwise,
especially when you're working

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:39.370
with hundreds, or even
thousands of resources

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:41.170
across many projects.

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:43.450
Idle VM and rightsizing
recommendations

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:45.460
help find resources
that aren't being used,

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:47.200
but that you're
still paying for.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:49.840
Even better, the unintended
project recommendations

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:53.000
would likely tell you which
projects aren't being used.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:55.150
And remember, recommendations
are exactly what they

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:57.243
sound like-- recommendations.

00:07:57.243 --> 00:07:59.410
We call these features
recommenders, because there's

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:00.785
no one better than
you at knowing

00:08:00.785 --> 00:08:02.620
the full details and
best steps to take,

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:04.270
and the recommendations
are only there

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:07.342
to help you identify
opportunities to dig deeper.

00:08:07.342 --> 00:08:08.800
You can check out
the documentation

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:10.930
to try these out for
yourself, and don't

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:12.730
forget about the
Recommendation hub, where

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:15.420
you can see all sorts of
helpful recommendations.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:16.920
Stay tuned for the
next video, where

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:18.370
we'll look at some more
recommendations that

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:20.620
will help you improve
another important factor--

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:21.700
performance.

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:23.670
See you there.

